{
  "id": "mg-inductance",
  "title": "Inductance & Mutual Inductance",
  "description": "Inductance and mutual inductance from the magnetic circuit, the dot convention and stored energy.",
  "reference": "Hambley §15.3–15.4",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Inductance from the magnetic circuit",
      "body": "Inductance is flux linkage per ampere. If all the flux links all $N$ turns, $\\lambda = N\\phi$ and $\\phi = Ni/\\mathcal{R}$, so $L = N^2/\\mathcal{R}$. Inductance grows with the **square** of the number of turns and is set by the core's dimensions and material. For a uniform core, $\\mathcal{R} = \\ell/(\\mu A)$ gives $L = N^2\\mu A/\\ell$. Faraday's law then gives the familiar $v = L\\,di/dt$.",
      "formula": "L = \\frac{\\lambda}{i} = \\frac{N\\phi}{i} = \\frac{N^2}{\\mathcal{R}}"
    },
    {
      "title": "Gapped cores",
      "body": "The permeability of iron varies with flux density, temperature and from sample to sample. Adding an air gap whose reluctance dominates makes $\\mathcal{R} \\approx \\ell_g/(\\mu_0A)$, so the inductance becomes predictable and the core saturates at a higher current. The price is a lower $L$ for the same number of turns.",
      "formula": "L \\approx \\frac{N^2\\mu_0A}{\\ell_g}\\quad(\\mathcal{R}_g \\gg \\mathcal{R}_c)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Mutual inductance",
      "body": "When two coils share a core, flux from coil 1 also links coil 2. The mutual inductance $M$ is the flux linkage of one coil per ampere in the other; it is the same both ways. If all flux links both coils, $M = N_1N_2/\\mathcal{R}$ and $M = \\sqrt{L_1L_2}$. In general the **coupling coefficient** $k$ is between 0 and 1.",
      "formula": "M = \\frac{\\lambda_{21}}{i_1} = \\frac{\\lambda_{12}}{i_2}, \\qquad k = \\frac{M}{\\sqrt{L_1L_2}} \\le 1"
    },
    {
      "title": "Dot convention",
      "body": "The dots mark the coil ends for which currents **entering** both dots produce **aiding** fluxes. With both currents entering the dots (as drawn) and passive reference polarities, the mutual terms are positive. If one current enters its dot and the other leaves its dot, the mutual terms change sign. An **increasing** $i_1$ entering a dot makes the dotted end of the other coil positive (a decreasing one makes it negative).",
      "formula": "v_1 = L_1\\frac{di_1}{dt} \\pm M\\frac{di_2}{dt}, \\qquad v_2 = \\pm M\\frac{di_1}{dt} + L_2\\frac{di_2}{dt}",
      "circuit": {
