    },
    {
      "title": "Coupled coils in series",
      "body": "Two coupled coils connected in series carry the same current. If the current enters both dotted ends (or leaves both), the fluxes aid and the mutual terms add. If it enters one dot and leaves the other, they subtract. Measuring both connections gives $M = (L_{aid} - L_{opp})/4$.",
      "formula": "L_{eq} = L_1 + L_2 \\pm 2M"
    },
    {
      "title": "Stored energy",
      "body": "An inductor carrying $i$ stores $W = \\tfrac12Li^2$ in its magnetic field, with energy density $B^2/(2\\mu)$ in a linear material. Because $\\mu_0$ is thousands of times smaller than iron's $\\mu$, almost all the energy of a gapped core sits in the **air gap**. For coupled coils the mutual term adds when the fluxes aid (both currents into dots) and subtracts otherwise.",
      "formula": "W = \\tfrac12L_1i_1^2 + \\tfrac12L_2i_2^2 \\pm Mi_1i_2"
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "mg-inductance-1",
      "title": "Self and mutual inductance",
      "kind": "learn",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "i1",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Where inductance comes from",
          "body": "A coil with $N$ turns on a core of reluctance $\\mathcal{R}$ makes a flux $\\phi = Ni/\\mathcal{R}$, and every turn links it. Inductance is flux linkage per ampere: $L = N\\phi/i$. Substituting the flux gives a formula that only depends on the turns and the core.",
          "formula": "L = \\frac{N^2}{\\mathcal{R}}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "N": [
              100,
              200,
              250,
              400,
              500
            ],
            "R": [
              100000.0,
              250000.0,
              500000.0,
              1000000.0,
              2000000.0
            ]
          },
          "prompt": "A coil of {{N}} turns is wound on a core with reluctance {{R|A·t/Wb}}. Find its inductance.",
          "answer": "N^2/R",
          "unit": "H",
          "explanation": "$L = \\frac{N^2}{\\mathcal{R}}$ = {{N}}² / {{R}} = {{N^2}} / {{R}} = {{N^2/R|H}}."
        },
        {
          "id": "q2",
          "type": "trueFalse",
          "prompt": "Doubling the number of turns on a core doubles the inductance.",
          "answer": false,
          "explanation": "False. $L = N^2/\\mathcal{R}$, so doubling $N$ multiplies $L$ by **four**: twice the mmf makes twice the flux, and that flux links twice as many turns."
        },
        {
          "id": "i2",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Mutual inductance",
          "body": "Put a second coil on the same core. The flux made by coil 1 now also links coil 2. Mutual inductance is the flux linkage of coil 2 per ampere in coil 1. If all flux links both coils:",
          "formula": "M = \\frac{N_2\\phi_1}{i_1} = \\frac{N_1N_2}{\\mathcal{R}}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q3",
          "type": "numeric",
          "params": {
            "N1": [
              100,
              200,
              300
            ],
            "N2": [
              50,
              100,
              400,
              600
            ],
            "R": [
              200000.0,
              500000.0,
              1000000.0
            ]
          },
          "prompt": "Coils of $N_1$ = {{N1}} and $N_2$ = {{N2}} turns share a core with reluctance {{R|A·t/Wb}}. All the flux links both coils.",
          "ask": [
            {
              "prompt": " Find the mutual inductance $M$.",
              "answer": "N1*N2/R",
              "unit": "H",
              "explanation": "$M = \\frac{N_1N_2}{\\mathcal{R}}$ = {{N1}} × {{N2}} / {{R}} = {{N1*N2/R|H}}. Check: $\\sqrt{L_1L_2}$ = $\\sqrt{({{N1^2/R}})({{N2^2/R}})}$ = {{N1*N2/R|H}} as well, since the coupling is perfect."
            },
            {
              "prompt": " Find the self-inductance $L_2$.",
              "answer": "N2^2/R",
              "unit": "H",
              "explanation": "$L_2 = \\frac{N_2^2}{\\mathcal{R}}$ = {{N2}}² / {{R}} = {{N2^2/R|H}}. (And $L_1$ = {{N1^2/R|H}}, $M$ = {{N1*N2/R|H}}.)"
            }
          ]
        },
